"sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Then the LORD said to Moses and Aaron,","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"“This is the statute of the law that the LORD has commanded: Instruct the Israelites to bring you an unblemished red heifer that has no defect and has never been placed under a yoke.","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"Anyone who touches a human corpse and fails to purify himself defiles the tabernacle of the LORD. That person must be cut off from Israel. He remains unclean, because the water of purification has not been sprinkled on him, and his uncleanness is still on him.","verse":13},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"But if a person who is unclean does not purify himself, he will be cut off from the assembly, because he has defiled the sanctuary of the LORD. The water of purification has not been sprinkled on him; he is unclean.","verse":20}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"Moses","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Moses::Moses::reviewed_unambiguous_name","sourceHeadwordId":"Moses"},"text":"Then the LORD said to Moses and Aaron,","verse":1}],"id":"Moses","label":"Moses","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Moses::Moses::reviewed_unambiguous_name","scope":"reviewed_unambiguous_name","senseReview":"Reviewed complete original name headword and source subject. Article covers the biblical person(s), named place, or named spiritual figure denoted by this exact spelling; multiple persons under one unchanged source article remain together. Actual proper-name capitalization and whole-name boundaries required. Explicit occurrence-level ambiguity exclusions override this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Moses","term":"Moses"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Moses","sourceTitle":"Moses"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"source_verified_redirect","phrase":"sin offering","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_publisher_redirect","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"OfferingsandSacrifices::Sin Offering::reviewed_publisher_redirect","sourceHeadwordId":"SinOffering"},"text":"For the purification of the unclean person, take some of the ashes of the burnt sin offering, put them in a jar, and pour fresh water over them.","verse":17}],"id":"OfferingsandSacrifices","label":"sin offering","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"OfferingsandSacrifices::Sin Offering::reviewed_publisher_redirect","scope":"reviewed_publisher_redirect","senseReview":"The original publisher explicitly links this complete unambiguous name/phrase to this target. Redirect and target were reviewed once; actual BSB phrase and full word boundaries required. Target original title and complete article remain unchanged.","sourceHeadwordId":"SinOffering","term":"Sin Offering"}],"matchingRule":"source_verified_redirect","matchingRules":["source_verified_redirect"],"sourceProvenanceId":"OfferingsandSacrifices","sourceTitle":"Offerings and Sacrifices"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"source_verified_redirect","phrase":"Tent of Meeting","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_publisher_redirect","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Tabernacle::Tent of Meeting::reviewed_publisher_redirect","sourceHeadwordId":"TentofMeeting"},"text":"Eleazar the priest is to take some of its blood on his finger and sprinkle it seven times toward the front of the Tent of Meeting.","verse":4}],"id":"Tabernacle","label":"Tent of Meeting","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Tabernacle::Tent of Meeting::reviewed_publisher_redirect","scope":"reviewed_publisher_redirect","senseReview":"The original publisher explicitly links this complete unambiguous name/phrase to this target. Redirect and target were reviewed once; actual BSB phrase and full word boundaries required. Target original title and complete article remain unchanged.","sourceHeadwordId":"TentofMeeting","term":"Tent of Meeting"}],"matchingRule":"source_verified_redirect","matchingRules":["source_verified_redirect"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Tabernacle","sourceTitle":"Tabernacle"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.1-22","r":"Num.19.1-22","rawR":"Num.19.1-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.1-22","value":"Num.19.1-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:1-22"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," This account considers the procedure by which ordinary Israelites defiled by contact with a corpse (see ",{"href":"?bref=Num.9.4-12","r":"Num.9.4-12","rawR":"Num.9.4-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.9.4-12","value":"Num.9.4-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"9:4-12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.17.15","r":"Lev.17.15","rawR":"Lev.17.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.17.15","value":"Lev.17.15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 17:15"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.21.1-4","r":"Lev.21.1-4","rawR":"Lev.21.1-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.21.1-4","value":"Lev.21.1-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"21:1-4"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.21.11-12","r":"Lev.21.11-12","rawR":"Lev.21.11-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.21.11-12","value":"Lev.21.11-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"11-12"},") could regain ceremonial purity and keep from defiling the Tabernacle. God’s holiness requires that the place where his presence dwells be kept pure and set apart from pollution (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=1Cor.6.9-11","r":"1Cor.6.9-11","rawR":"1Cor.6.9-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Cor.6.9-11","value":"1Cor.6.9-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Cor 6:9-11"},", ",{"href":"?bref=1Cor.6.18-20","r":"1Cor.6.18-20","rawR":"1Cor.6.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Cor.6.18-20","value":"1Cor.6.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"18-20"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.1-22","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":22,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.1-22","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"e4b65880c691be4c82c13cc7703c65951c064419831547701e842309c209d201","sourceItemName":"Num.19.1-22","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1805,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.1-22","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.1-22","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.1-10","r":"Num.19.1-10","rawR":"Num.19.1-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.1-10","value":"Num.19.1-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:1-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["water for the purification ceremony"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=Num.19.9","r":"Num.19.9","rawR":"Num.19.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.9","value":"Num.19.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:9"},"), prepared with ashes of the red heifer, may have been the same as the water of purification in ",{"href":"?bref=Num.8.7","r":"Num.8.7","rawR":"Num.8.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.8.7","value":"Num.8.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"8:7"},"; see also ",{"href":"?bref=Num.31.19-24","r":"Num.31.19-24","rawR":"Num.31.19-24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.31.19-24","value":"Num.31.19-24"}],"t":"ref","v":"31:19-24"},". This ceremony required the ",{"c":["ashes"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of a ",{"c":["red heifer;"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," the color probably represented blood (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.9.13-14","r":"Heb.9.13-14","rawR":"Heb.9.13-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.9.13-14","value":"Heb.9.13-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 9:13-14"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.1-10","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.1-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"bd55c8c88246d65b375c2f6c3ab9bfc676df750a3b9664018cc03333f7d047a3","sourceItemName":"Num.19.1-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1806,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.1-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.1-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.3-5","r":"Num.19.3-5","rawR":"Num.19.3-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.3-5","value":"Num.19.3-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:3-5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The need to safeguard Aaron’s consecrated status (see ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.21.10-12","r":"Lev.21.10-12","rawR":"Lev.21.10-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.21.10-12","value":"Lev.21.10-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 21:10-12"},") required that ",{"c":["Eleazar"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," perform the offering of the red ",{"c":["heifer"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," since it caused ritual impurity. The ceremony of the red heifer contrasted with the regular sacrifice (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.4.3-12","r":"Lev.4.3-12","rawR":"Lev.4.3-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.4.3-12","value":"Lev.4.3-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 4:3-12"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.3-5","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.3-5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c66628e84ad1261edf66040af699ae7435f2a9f043ab6650ba9c805f14ec4b61","sourceItemName":"Num.19.3-5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1807,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.3-5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.3-5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.6","r":"Num.19.6","rawR":"Num.19.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.6","value":"Num.19.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:6"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The only other passage where ",{"c":["a stick of cedar, a hyssop branch, and some scarlet yarn"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," occur together is ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.14.1-57","r":"Lev.14.1-57","rawR":"Lev.14.1-57","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.14.1-57","value":"Lev.14.1-57"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 14"},", which describes another ceremony (also “outside the camp”) that brought about ceremonial purification. Some scholars suggest that Eleazar added cedarwood and hyssop to produce an aroma. Hyssop is associated with purification (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.12.22","r":"Exod.12.22","rawR":"Exod.12.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.12.22","value":"Exod.12.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 12:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ps.51.7","r":"Ps.51.7","rawR":"Ps.51.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ps.51.7","value":"Ps.51.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ps 51:7"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Heb.9.19","r":"Heb.9.19","rawR":"Heb.9.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Heb.9.19","value":"Heb.9.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Heb 9:19"},"), and the scarlet yarn, like the red heifer, probably symbolized blood. Taken together, the cedarwood, hyssop, and scarlet symbolize cleansing."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.6","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":6,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":6}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.6","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"c4f70e1ee9c338fceb4373b0962475d06cdff33d24137dcc945220a5d1160221","sourceItemName":"Num.19.6","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1808,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.6","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.6","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.7-10","r":"Num.19.7-10","rawR":"Num.19.7-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.7-10","value":"Num.19.7-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:7-10"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Three people were needed to produce and collect the heifer’s ashes—Eleazar, the man who burned the animal, and the man who gathered its ashes. All three became ritually impure for a day because of this process. This was also true for the person who used the finished product, a potion made from the ash dust and water (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Num.19.21","r":"Num.19.21","rawR":"Num.19.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.21","value":"Num.19.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:21"},") that had the same defiling effect as blood associated with sacrifices (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.6.27-28","r":"Lev.6.27-28","rawR":"Lev.6.27-28","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.6.27-28","value":"Lev.6.27-28"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 6:27-28"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.7-10","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":10,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.7-10","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"75b86bd730c6a76abe5c871a22ea8ee4cd8a015eef857b9b3653cad44e37c95d","sourceItemName":"Num.19.7-10","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1809,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.7-10","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.7-10","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.9","r":"Num.19.9","rawR":"Num.19.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.9","value":"Num.19.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:9"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The purpose of the ceremony of burning the red heifer was to produce ",{"c":["ashes"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},". These were collected and deposited ",{"c":["in a purified place outside the camp"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," until the Israelites needed them ",{"c":["in the water for the purification ceremony"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," to remove such defilement as coming into contact with a corpse (",{"href":"?bref=Num.19.11-22","r":"Num.19.11-22","rawR":"Num.19.11-22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.11-22","value":"Num.19.11-22"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:11-22"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.9","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":9,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":9}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.9","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"8b2fde45341048391a3c7c845239965cf635ae74915cd99234d98ff2dcf1bf44","sourceItemName":"Num.19.9","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1810,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.9","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.9","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.11-19","r":"Num.19.11-19","rawR":"Num.19.11-19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.11-19","value":"Num.19.11-19"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:11-19"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Coming into contact with a human corpse produced ritual impurity for ",{"c":["seven days"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.21.1-3","r":"Lev.21.1-3","rawR":"Lev.21.1-3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.21.1-3","value":"Lev.21.1-3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 21:1-3"},"). The remedy was the ",{"c":["water of purification"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," applied ",{"c":["on the third and seventh days"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Num.19.11-19","refs":[{"book":"NUM","endChapter":19,"endVerse":19,"startChapter":19,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Num.19.11-19","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"NUM","sourceChapterCoverage":[19],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"fdcd9186f78d0c6d6312ca566a80cc440e5d07bcb3351513ecff58d09873301f","sourceItemName":"Num.19.11-19","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1811,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"NUM","canonicalOSIS":"Num","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Num"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Num.19.11-19","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Num.19.11-19","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Num.19.13","r":"Num.19.13","rawR":"Num.19.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.19.13","value":"Num.19.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"19:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The impurity caused by contact with a corpse was especially dangerous since a ceremonially defiled person would ",{"c":["defile the"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Lord"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt-divine-name","t":"span"},{"c":["’s Tabernacle"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.15.31","r":"Lev.15.31","rawR":"Lev.15.31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.15.31","value":"Lev.15.31"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 15:31"},"). This was grounds for being ",{"c":["cut off from the community of Israel"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Num.9.13","r":"Num.9.13","rawR":"Num.9.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.9.13","value":"Num.9.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 9:13"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.12.15","r":"Exod.12.15","rawR":"Exod.12.15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.1